{
  "dom_id": "step-q334011",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given the set of functions ${\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning. For all $x\\in R,$ we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
